.sass-cache/

# Unpublished. One entry per site's drafts collection — the pattern is the
# collection's source directory name, so RENAMING A COLLECTION SILENTLY
# UN-IGNORES IT. That happened once: `_drafts/` became `_food_drafts/` in the
# mono-repo migration and 221 unpublished recipes stopped being ignored without
# a word. tests/test_site_config.py now checks this against _config.yml.
#
# THE TRAILING SLASH IS NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN, found 2026-08-27. `foo/` matches
# a DIRECTORY, and a symlink to a directory is not one as far as git is
# concerned -- so `_cocktail_drafts` linked into a worktree from the main
# checkout showed up as an untracked path that `git add -A` would have staged.
# The slashless twin covers that; both forms are kept because
# test_every_drafts_collection_is_gitignored requires the `_<name>/` shape, and
# the slash is what stops the pattern also matching a stray FILE of that name.
_food_drafts/
_food_drafts
_cocktail_drafts/
_cocktail_drafts

# Individual pages held back the same way as the collections above — not
# ready for a public repo, regardless of what Jekyll would or wouldn't build.
# `published: false` in the page's own front matter is a SEPARATE, unrelated
# switch (controls what Jekyll renders); it does nothing about git. This is
# the entry that actually keeps the file off GitHub.
# food/about.html

